  "findings": {
    "samplerAdoption": "Verified for this installed binary: innermost process environment always_off produced 0 spans/17 logs, always_on 513 spans/17 logs on same synthetic HTTP scenario. This establishes sampler configuration adoption, not lossless export.",
    "flush": "Not externally provable as a complete census with current public native interface. Explicit collector HTTP400 on every received batch still permits exit0/valid final; native stderr contains no exporter warning. Audited native shutdown ignores exporter shutdown errors. No terminal export sequence/count receipt exists in observed OTLP.",
    "receiverSealing": "A private receiver can durably seal bytes it actually received after its own writes drain, but cannot certify absence of dropped native batches. Sender completion acknowledgment/count would be necessary for global completeness. Quiet period or process exit alone never supplies that.",
    "websocket": "Actual free WS upgrades succeeded across ordinary exec/resume same session. Two real sampling requests each parent-link to a usage span10input/2output. Two additional WS request logs are startup/prewarm outside sampling attempts; do not count them as billed model generations. Wire fixture confirms generate=false prewarm.",
    "unknowns": [
      "Real ChatGPT auth/workspace routing and TLS remain untested.",
      "Custom fake WS success does not prove coverage of a production WS fallback/recovery path.",
      "No provider response ID in these sampled usage associations; mapping is native attempt-tree evidence, not response-level invoice reconciliation.",
      "Native silent drop before export can still be invisible. Actual billed usage remains unknown; valid provider-reported records stay usable as reported records.",
      "P158 no-stdout-error incident is not reproduced or explained by this experiment."
    ]
